C = [
("h1", "4. “The Figure of Him That Was to Come”"),
("p", "Claim: the last clause of Romans 5:14 is the hinge of the passage. Adam “is the figure of him that was to come,” and the verses that follow say in what the figure holds and in what it does not. Paul states the likeness in one sentence and the unlikeness in three. A reader who keeps the proportions will see that the comparison is made so that the difference may be seen."),
("sub", "4.1 What a figure is"),
("p", "The word the King James Version renders “figure” is a word for an impression, such as a stamp leaves in wax, and so for a pattern or an example. In Romans 5:14 it is used of a man. Adam is the pattern, and the one who was to come is the one who fills it. The clause looks forward. Adam is not called a figure of himself or of his descendants. He is called a figure of the one who was to come, and the volume takes the phrase to name Jesus Christ, as the sentence from verse 15 onward does."),
("p", "A figure is not a copy. A seal in wax has the shape of the seal reversed, and the likeness is in the outline, not in the substance. Paul does not say that Adam’s act and Christ’s act are the same sort of act, or of the same weight, or that they do the same thing. He says that Adam is the figure, and the next verses are devoted to saying how far the figure reaches."),
("sub", "4.2 The likeness"),
("p", "The likeness is stated by the repeated word “one.” In the King James text the word “one” occurs twelve times between verse 12 and verse 19. One man brings sin into the world, and one man is the source of the gift. One offence leads to judgment, and one righteousness leads to justification. One man’s disobedience leads to the many being made sinners, and the obedience of one leads to the many being made righteous (Romans 5:12, 15–19). The figure is the shape of the sentence itself: one stands for the many."),
("p", "The likeness is also in the word “many.” Paul says that “through the offence of one many be dead” (Romans 5:15) and that “by one man’s disobedience many were made sinners, so by the obedience of one shall many be made righteous” (Romans 5:19). Whatever “many” comes to mean in each half of the sentence, the shape of the claim is the same. A result that begins with one man reaches many."),
("sub", "4.3 The unlikeness: three “not as” clauses"),
("p", "Paul does not let the figure stand without correction. Immediately after the verse that says Adam is the figure of him that was to come, he writes three sentences that mark the difference. They have a plain form. Each begins with a denial of equality, and each is followed by the reason."),
("ctbl", "Likeness and unlikeness in Romans 5:15–17.", [
    ("Verse", "The correction", "What it shows"),
    ("5:15", "“But not as the offence, so also is the free gift.” The gift is “much more” than the offence: “if through the offence of one many be dead, much more the grace of God, and the gift by grace, which is by one man, Jesus Christ, hath abounded unto many.”", "The two acts are not equal. The gift abounds beyond the offence."),
    ("5:16", "“And not as it was by one that sinned, so is the gift: for the judgment was by one to condemnation, but the free gift is of many offences unto justification.”", "One offence led to judgment; the gift answers many offences and leads to justification."),
    ("5:17", "“For if by one man’s offence death reigned by one; much more they which receive abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness shall reign in life by one, Jesus Christ.”", "Death reigned by one; those who receive reign in life by one. The second half carries a condition: “they which receive.”"),
  ], [0.10, 0.58, 0.32]),
("p", "Three differences are named. The first is one of size: the gift is “much more” than the offence. The second is one of kind: the offence is followed by judgment unto condemnation, but the gift answers “many offences” and ends in justification. The third is one of means: death “reigned by one,” but the second half of the sentence speaks of “they which receive abundance of grace.” In the first half nothing is said of anything the many do to come under death’s reign. In the second half the many are those who “receive.”"),
("sub", "4.4 What follows from the third difference"),
("p", "The third difference carries the weight in the chapters that follow, and it is stated here so that the reader may hold it. The figure holds in the shape: one man, many affected. It does not hold in the mode. Paul does not say that the many come under Adam’s result by receiving anything. He does say that those who come under the second man’s result are those who “receive.” The word is the word that was already in Romans 5:11, “by whom we have now received the atonement.”"),
("p", "This is the first place in the passage where the scope of the second headship is given in Paul’s own words. The volume reads it as such, and does not hurry past it. The means of reception is not set in Romans 5. It is set in the chapters before, where Paul has been speaking of faith (Romans 3:22, 28; 4:5; 5:1). The volume leaves those chapters where they are and asks only what Romans 5:17 itself says: that the one who comes under the second man’s result is the one who receives."),
("sub", "4.5 “Him that was to come”"),
("p", "The phrase also says something about time. Adam is the figure of one who “was to come,” and the verb looks ahead from Adam’s place in the record to a man who had not yet appeared. Paul writes as one for whom the man has come. The cross has been spoken of in the verses just read, and the gift is called “the free gift” in the past tense of an event. The figure and the thing figured are on opposite sides of the cross."),
("p", "A last observation concerns the name. Paul names Adam here by name. He names the second man only as “one man, Jesus Christ” (Romans 5:15) and “one, Jesus Christ” (Romans 5:17). He does not call him “the last Adam” in this passage. That name belongs to the other passage, and it is a name given for a different purpose."),
]
